::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Expand log_2(xy).
{
=A. log_2(x) + log_2(y)#Correct. The product property changes multiplication inside one logarithm into addition of logarithms.
~B. log_2(x) - log_2(y)#Subtraction is used for quotients, not products.
~C. log_2(x + y)#This changes the expression and does not apply a log property.
~D. 2log(xy)#This does not represent the product property.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Condense log_5(a) - log_5(b).
{
~A. log_5(ab)#That would match addition of logarithms.
=B. log_5(a/b)#Correct. The quotient property changes subtraction of same-base logarithms into division inside one logarithm.
~C. log_5(a - b)#There is no subtraction property inside a logarithm.
~D. log_5(b/a)#This reverses the quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Rewrite 4log_3(x) as one logarithm.
{
~A. log_3(4x)#The coefficient becomes an exponent, not a factor.
~B. log_3(x + 4)#There is no addition property like this.
=C. log_3(x^4)#Correct. The power property moves the coefficient to an exponent inside the logarithm.
~D. 4 + log_3(x)#This is not equivalent to multiplying the logarithm by 4.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which expression is equivalent to log_7(m/n)?
{
~A. log_7(m) + log_7(n)#Addition corresponds to multiplication.
~B. log_7(m - n)#Subtraction inside a log does not split.
~C. log_7(n) - log_7(m)#This reverses the quotient.
=D. log_7(m) - log_7(n)#Correct. The quotient property separates division into subtraction.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.LOG.4
Question:
Which rewrite is invalid?
{
=A. log_2(x + y) = log_2(x) + log_2(y)#Correct. Logarithms do not split over addition inside the argument.
~B. log_2(xy) = log_2(x) + log_2(y)#This is the product property.
~C. log_2(x/y) = log_2(x) - log_2(y)#This is the quotient property.
~D. log_2(x^3) = 3log_2(x)#This is the power property.
}

